      "criterion": "docs/07-lane-policy.md defines version AUTHORITY as the mechanism that selects a package's severity and baseline for a given planning point, and names, for every package, exactly one such authority: the package itself (independent), its fixed group, its linked group, or the workspace's prerelease lane (as defined by the roster in .changeset/pre.json's initialVersions). The document states the precedence for all six unordered pairs among these four authority kinds, with no pairing left undecided: (1) independent x fixed group -- mutually exclusive by construction: GroupTable::resolve (crates/callisto-graph/src/config/groups.rs:127-222) tracks every package claimed by any fixed or linked group in a single shared `claimed_by: BTreeMap<PackageId, GroupName>` map (declared at crates/callisto-graph/src/config/groups.rs:132) spanning both the fixed resolution loop (which checks `claimed_by` at line 138 and inserts at line 146 before writing `fixed_of` at line 149) and the linked resolution loop (which checks at line 179 and inserts at line 187 before writing `linked_of` at line 190), so a package present in `fixed_of` is necessarily absent from the independent set, which is defined as the packages having neither a `fixed_of` nor a `linked_of` entry (crates/callisto-graph/src/config/groups.rs:14-15). There is no `ungrouped` collection in the codebase; independence is defined by the absence of both entries, not by a third container. (2) independent x linked group -- mutually exclusive by the same construction. (3) fixed group x linked group -- mutually exclusive: the shared `claimed_by` map means a package claimed by a fixed group that is also named in a linked group's member list is rejected at config-resolution time via GraphError::ConflictingGroupMembership (E109, crates/callisto-graph/src/error.rs:114), per AC-002, before any planning proceeds -- this holds even when the colliding raw member names differ syntactically (e.g. a bare name vs. a scoped alias resolving to the same PackageId via IdentityIndex::resolve_human), not only for literal string duplicates, because the map is keyed by resolved PackageId. (4) independent x prerelease lane -- MAY apply concurrently: an independent package (one with neither `fixed_of` nor `linked_of` set) may be on the prerelease roster; when the lane is active, its mode (PreMode::Pre or PreMode::Exit) takes precedence over independent inference for shaping the package's target version (prerelease tag application while accumulating, or finalization to stable on exit), though the package's own inferred severity still feeds the lane's bump as input. (5) fixed group x prerelease lane -- MUST NOT apply concurrently; rejected per AC-002a. (6) linked group x prerelease lane -- MUST NOT apply concurrently; rejected per AC-002a. The document states as a corollary that a package's prerelease-ROSTER membership can only ever coexist with the independent authority kind, never with fixed or linked group membership, because (5) and (6) are hard rejections. The document additionally states, as an explicit carve-out from the authority model above, that workspace-wide prerelease SHAPING is not an authority in this sense: in PreMode::Pre, `bump_target` (crates/callisto-graph/src/cascade.rs:428-433) applies `bump_prerelease` with the lane's tag to every package it is called on regardless of roster membership, but it selects neither the package's severity nor its group -- it only re-shapes the version that the package's own authority already selected. AC-003b case (4a) is the normative statement of that shaping behavior, and the roster/shaping gap it creates for an off-roster group member is recorded in non_goals rather than closed by this spec.",
